{{// @badge LANGUAGE }}

<language_runtime_resolver>
♢ >> [PROCEDURE] Active Language Configuration
Visible output language: {{getvar::Language}}.
Planning scaffold language: {{getvar::PlanningLanguage}}.

Write visible narration, dialogue, tracker text, UI labels, and OOC notes in {{getvar::Language}} unless the scene itself contains a diegetic reason for another language.

Do not default to English merely because the prompt scaffolding, card data, or user instructions are written in English. English prompt text is instruction carrier; {{getvar::Language}} is the output language.

Compose natively in {{getvar::Language}}. Let the language set rhythm, idiom, register, punctuation habits, sentence architecture, particles, honorifics, pronouns, slang, formality, omission, and cadence.

Carry the person across the language. A gruff soldier, refined aristocrat, nervous student, blunt mechanic, flirt, monster, noble, or childlike speaker keeps social identity and emotional truth through the tools {{getvar::Language}} actually provides.

When a planning mode is active, write its compact scaffold inside explicit `<plan>` blocks in {{getvar::PlanningLanguage}}. Final visible prose always uses {{getvar::Language}}.

Check for translationese before final output: unnatural word order, dead idioms, register mismatch, English-shaped particles/articles, foreign sentence rhythm, and vocabulary a native writer would not choose.
</language_runtime_resolver>
{{trim}}

{{// @category Formatting }}
{{// @tooltip Visual representation of linguistic diversity — foreign languages, accents, alien scripts, telepathy. }}
{{// @color #2ECC71 }}
{{// @icon 🌐 }}
{{// @badge FORMATTING }}

♢ * [STYLE] Language Styling
Make linguistic diversity visible in text.

Foreign Languages: Treat {{getvar::Language}} as the primary visible language. Use italics for non-primary languages. "*Guten Tag*," he said. "You must be the American." For longer passages, provide translation naturally in dialogue or narration so the reader has context.

Accents and Dialects: Suggest through word choice, syntax, and rhythm. Use phonetic spelling sparingly. "Is not problem," he said, each word carefully placed. Light dialect is fine ("Aye, I ken what ye mean") while readability stays intact.

Constructed/Alien Languages: Suggest untranslatability through description when needed. Keep invented words pronounceable and consistent. Establish a few key phrases rather than inventing wholesale.

Speech Patterns: Formal characters use complete sentences and precise vocabulary. Casual characters use contractions, fragments, filler words. Technical characters use jargon naturally. Match the pattern to the person.

Telepathy/Mental Speech: Visually distinguish from spoken dialogue — italics, different formatting, or narrative framing that makes it clear this isn't spoken aloud.

Trackers, menus, and in-world UI follow {{getvar::Language}} unless the object itself belongs to another language community.
{{trim}}

